DP  - 2025 Nov 12
TI  - Acute respiratory distress syndrome associated with e-cigarette or vaping product 
      use in Bogotá, Colombia: a lung injury epidemic.
PG  - 521
LID - 10.1186/s12890-025-03972-8 [doi]
LID - 521
AB  - BACKGROUND: E-cigarette or vaping product use-associated lung injury (EVALI) 
      emerged as a serious public health concern following outbreaks in North America, 
      but remains underreported in Latin America. This study aims to describe the 
      clinical, radiologic, and histopathologic features of EVALI in a tertiary care 
      center in Bogotá, Colombia. METHODS: We conducted a retrospective case series of 
      patients hospitalized with suspected EVALI between January 2020 and July 2024. 
      Inclusion criteria followed CDC guidelines: history of vaping in the last 90 
      days, radiographic abnormalities (notably ground-glass opacities), and exclusion 
      of alternative diagnoses. We analyzed demographic, clinical, imaging, and 
      histopathologic data, including ICU admissions and outcomes. RESULTS: Twenty-five 
      patients met CDC criteria for EVALI. The mean age was 30 years; 80% were male. 
      Most (72%) reported exclusive e-cigarette use, primarily nicotine-based. Informal 
      sourcing of vaping liquids was reported in 28%. The most common symptoms were 
      cough (88%), dyspnea (80%), and fever (76%). Imaging revealed bilateral 
      ground-glass opacities in 88%. ICU admission was required in 76%, and 8% needed 
      mechanical ventilation. Bronchoscopy and/or lung biopsy confirmed organizing 
      pneumonia or acute interstitial inflammation in selected cases. All patients 
      received corticosteroids with clinical and radiological recovery; no deaths 
      occurred, however causality cannot be inferred from this retrospective series. 
      CONCLUSIONS: This is one of the largest EVALI case series reported in Latin 
      America. Our findings underscore the importance of early recognition and 
      management of EVALI in low- and middle-income countries, particularly amid 
      increasing e-cigarette use among youth. Public health policies must address 
      informal vape markets and misinformation surrounding product safety.
