TI  - 2000 Peripheral Pulmonary Lesions Sampled by Shape-Sensing Robotic-Assisted 
      Bronchoscopy and Mobile Cone-Beam Computed Tomography: The Mayo Clinic 
      Experience.
PG  - 568-581
LID - S0025-6196(25)16630-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.015 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To assess the 5-year experience using shape-sensing robotic-assisted 
      bronchoscopy (ssRAB) in three academic centers following the strict diagnostic 
      yield (DY) consensus, including concurrent mediastinal staging with endobronchial 
      ultrasound transbronchial needle aspiration. PATIENTS AND METHODS: A multicenter 
      study on consecutive patients who underwent ssRAB for evaluation of suspicious 
      peripheral pulmonary lesions from July 2019 to August 2024 across the three Mayo 
      Clinic sites. The primary outcome included DY and the analysis of variables 
      associated with it based on the strict definition of DY. Secondary outcomes were 
      intermediate DY, safety, and the potential impact on the streamlining of care for 
      patients with suspicious lung lesions. RESULTS: A total of 2115 peripheral 
      pulmonary lesions were biopsied using ssRAB from 1904 patients. Strict DY was 
      76.9% and intermediate DY was 80.2%. Sensitivity for malignancy was 85.0% and 
      staging with endobronchial ultrasound transbronchial needle aspiration was 
      concurrently performed in 74% of patients. The 1.1-mm cryoprobe (odds ratio [OR], 
      1.88; 95% CI, 1.42 to 2.49; P<.001), solid density on computed tomography (OR, 
      1.42; 95% CI, 1.06 to 1.89; P=.02), maximum diameter (OR, 1.77; 95% CI, 1.43 to 
      2.19; P<.001), and body mass index (OR, 0.95; 95% CI, 0.95 to 0.98; P<.001), were 
      significant predictors of DY. The rate of adverse events requiring intervention 
      was 2.8%. Early lung cancer detection increased from 46% in 2019 to 69% in 2024, 
      whereas local/advanced diagnosis decreased from 54% to 31% in 2024. CONCLUSION: 
      In a high-volume multicenter setting, ssRAB showed a consistently high diagnostic 
      performance, particularly for detecting malignancy, and has improved early lung 
      cancer diagnosis with a low complication rate.
