DP  - 2025 Oct 12
TI  - [Residual neuromuscular blockade during recovery from anesthesia in respiratory 
      endoscopy: a report of three cases and literature review].
PG  - 937-942
LID - 10.3760/cma.j.cn112147-20250508-00252 [doi]
AB  - Objective: To investigate the clinical risks and preventive strategies of 
      residual neuromuscular blockade during the recovery period after general 
      anesthesia for respiratory endoscopy. Methods: A retrospective analysis of 3 
      cases of respiratory endoscopy under intravenous general anesthesia was conducted 
      at Chuiyangliu Hospital Affiliated to Tsinghua University in 2024. Relevant 
      literature was searched in the Pubmed and Wanfang database to summarize and 
      analyze the clinical manifestations and risks. Results: The 3 patients at our 
      hospital underwent thorough evaluation before operation. Sedation and analgesia 
      were administered, along with 0.1-0.2 mg/kg dose of the neuromuscular blocking 
      agent succinylcholine. The bronchoscopy procedure was successful. Neostigmine was 
      administered for antagonism after bronchoscopy in all 3 cases, but adverse events 
      occurred during anesthesia recovery. The first patient experienced persistent 
      chest tightness, despite normal results from auxiliary examination, and 
      psychological testing suggested an anxiety state, which improved after 
      psychological intervention. The second patient developed respiratory and heart 
      failure but was successfully resuscitated. The third patient experienced 
      prolonged hypoxemia during the recovery period from anesthesia. Literature 
      analysis showed that residual neuromuscular block during the recovery period can 
      manifest clinically as chest tightness, respiratory muscle weakness, hypoxemia, 
      respiratory failure and heart failure. Residual neuromuscular block is common and 
      causes severe adverse clinical effects. However, despite its serious clinical 
      impact, residual neuromuscular block has not attracted much clinical attention. 
      Conclusion: The clinical hazards associated with residual neuromuscular blockade 
      are not widely recognized, and effective preventive strategies must be developed 
      to mitigate these risks.
