TI  - [Bronchoscopic airway stent placement combined with vaseline gauze packing for 
      refractory bronchopleural fistula:a report of 22 cases].
PG  - 668-672
LID - 10.3760/cma.j.cn112147-20260302-00116 [doi]
AB  - A retrospective analysis was conducted of the clinical data of 22 patients with 
      refractory bronchopleural fistula admitted to the Department of Respiratory and 
      Critical Care Medicine, Emergency General Hospital, from January 2024 to January 
      2025.All patients underwent bronchoscopic airway stent placement combined with 
      petroleum jelly gauze packing. Of these, 18 cases (81.8%) had central-type 
      fistulas (mean diameter:5.22±1.96 mm), and 4 cases (18.2%) had peripheral-type 
      fistulas (mean diameter:1.38±0.75 mm).Twenty patients had a single fistula, and 
      two had multiple fistulas; 14 patients (63.6%) had concomitant empyema. Dyspnea 
      and Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) scores were compared before and after 
      treatment, and time to residual cavity closure, time to chest tube removal, and 
      complications were recorded. During 6 to 12-month follow-up, 20 patients achieved 
      residual cavity closure, with a median closure time of 2.5 months; the median 
      time to chest tube removal was 3 months. Both dyspnea and KPS scores showed 
      improved significantly compared to pre-treatment values. Treatment failed in two 
      patients::one was converted to open-window drainage, and one died of an unrelated 
      illness 9 months postoperatively. One patient developed severe granulation tissue 
      hyperplasia postoperatively, which improved after stent removal. These 
      preliminary findings suggest that bronchoscopic airway stent placement combined 
      with Vaseline gauze packing is an effective and safe minimally invasive 
      interventional treatment for refractory bronchopleural fistula.
