TI  - Application of hand-drawn bronchial navigation and rapid on-site evaluation 
      combined with endobronchial ultrasound for the diagnosis of peripheral lung 
      lesions.
PG  - 3000605261468572
LID - 10.1177/03000605261468572 [doi]
LID - 03000605261468572
AB  - ObjectiveTo investigate the clinical value of radial endobronchial ultrasound 
      (rEBUS) combined with rapid on-site evaluation (ROSE) under hand-drawn bronchial 
      navigation (HBN) planning for the diagnosis of peripheral pulmonary 
      lesions.MethodsA total of 186 patients were enrolled in this retrospective 
      case-control study and classified into three groups according to the intervention 
      method: rEBUS group (n = 74), HBN-rEBUS group (n = 57), and HBN-rEBUS-ROSE group 
      (n = 54). The final diagnostic yield, lesion detection rate, time to lesion 
      localization, number of biopsies performed, and incidence of complications were 
      compared across the three groups.ResultsThe lesion detection rate was higher in 
      the HBN-rEBUS group than in the rEBUS group, and the HBN-rEBUS group demonstrated 
      fewer ultrasound probe passes and a shorter time to lesion localization 
      (p < 0.001). The HBN-rEBUS-ROSE group required fewer biopsies and had a lower 
      incidence of bleeding than the HBN-rEBUS group (p < 0.001). The HBN-rEBUS-ROSE 
      group also achieved a higher diagnostic yield than both the rEBUS and HBN-rEBUS 
      groups (p < 0.05).ConclusionHBN-rEBUS facilitates faster and more precise 
      localization of peripheral pulmonary lesions, shortens procedural duration, and, 
      when combined with ROSE, reduces bleeding and improves the diagnostic yield of 
      peripheral pulmonary lesions.
