TI  - When history can mislead a physician: a challenging case report of idiopathic 
      pulmonary hemosiderosis-A case report and review of the literature.
LID - 10.1186/s13256-026-05878-3 [doi]
LID - 351
AB  - BACKGROUND: Idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis is a rare condition characterized 
      by non-immune-mediated alveolar hemorrhage. Its nonspecific clinical and 
      radiological features make diagnosis particularly challenging. Idiopathic 
      pulmonary hemosiderosis typically presents in older children with a classic triad 
      of hemoptysis, chest X-ray abnormalities, and iron deficiency anemia. In 
      contrast, preschool-aged children may show subtle signs such as respiratory 
      distress, persistent cough, and pallor, which can mimic respiratory infections 
      and lead to severe anemia. Prompt recognition is critical, as iron deposition in 
      the alveoli adversely affects prognosis. Delayed diagnosis may result in 
      pulmonary fibrosis, respiratory failure, or the need for lung transplantation. 
      CASE PRESENTATION: We report the case of a 3-year-old Asian girl with no 
      significant medical history who presented with respiratory distress and severe 
      anemia. She was initially treated as pneumonia with nutritional iron deficiency 
      anemia, received a blood transfusion, and was discharged on iron and folic acid 
      supplements; 2 weeks later, she returned with worsening respiratory distress, 
      pallor, and hypoactivity. Workup revealed severe microcytic hypochromic anemia, 
      elevated inflammatory markers, and bilateral heterogeneous opacities on chest 
      X-ray. Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage was suspected and confirmed by bronchoscopy 
      after exclusion of rheumatological diseases. Idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis 
      was considered, and she was started on pulse methylprednisolone for 3 days, then 
      weaned to maintenance prednisolone doses. Empirical antibiotics were discontinued 
      after negative infectious studies. She showed significant clinical and 
      radiological improvement within days, and at 2-month follow-up her hemoglobin was 
      11.2 g/dL. CONCLUSION: This case highlights the risk of misdiagnosing rare but 
      serious conditions such as idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis. Early recognition 
      and timely treatment are essential to improve outcomes and prevent long-term 
      complications.
