TI  - Elevated Squamous Cell Carcinoma Associated Antigen and Pulmonary Nodule Caused 
      by Airway Foreign Bodies Mimic Lung Cancer.
LID - 10.7754/Clin.Lab.2025.250145 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Airway foreign bodies can occur at any age and often expose the 
      patients to the risk of pulmonary infections, pulmonary atelectasis, and even 
      asphyxia. It is more commonly observed in children and less likely in adults. We 
      report a case of an elderly man with elevated squamous cell carcinoma-associated 
      antigen combined with multiple nodules in both lungs, initially diagnosed as lung 
      cancer and eventually diagnosed as multiple foreign bodies in both lungs by 
      bronchoscopy. METHODS: Appropriate laboratory tests, chest enhanced CT, and 
      bronchoscopy. RESULTS: Squamous cell carcinoma-associated antigen (SCC) was 
      elevated to 6.73 ng/mL, and an enhanced chest CT showed multiple nodular ground 
      glass shadows in both lungs. Bronchoscopy indicated that smooth green foreign 
      bodies were visible in the opening of the upper lobe of the left lung, smooth red 
      foreign bodies were visible in the opening of the lower lobe of the right lung, 
      irregular white foreign bodies were visible in the opening of the anterior 
      segment of the upper lobe of the right lung, and the surrounding granulation 
      tissue was hyperplasia. After the removal of the foreign body, the airway became 
      unobstructed, and the patient's blood oxygen saturation was significantly 
      improved. CONCLUSIONS: The clinical manifestation of tracheobronchial foreign 
      body in the elderly are not typical, so they are often missed or misdiagnosed. 
      The bronchoscope is very important in diagnosing and treating the trachea and 
      bronchial foreign body. At the same time, it is also important to promote 
      measures to prevent food inhalation in the family for the elderly who are at the 
      wrong risk of inhalation.
