TI  - Echoes of infection: radial EBUS unveils invasive pulmonary aspergillosis.
LID - e264471 [pii]
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2024-264471 [doi]
AB  - Invasive fungal disease commonly affects immunocompromised hosts and rarely 
      immunocompetent individuals. The disease carries high mortality, making early 
      diagnosis and initiation of treatment crucial in the management. Diagnosis is 
      based on host, clinical, mycological and histological factors. Bronchoscopy, 
      especially radial endobronchial ultrasound (REBUS), is a widely used technique in 
      the evaluation of peripheral pulmonary lesions. Here, we present an interesting 
      case of an elderly man in his late 60s with pemphigus foliaceus on 
      immunosuppressants who presented with fever and cough for 1 week, with CT of the 
      chest showing consolidations in both lungs. REBUS-guided biopsy from the right 
      upper lobe with bronchoalveolar lavage revealed the diagnosis of invasive 
      pulmonary aspergillosis. The patient improved with the initiation of antifungal 
      therapy. This case highlights the importance of REBUS-guided targeted sampling of 
      lung while suspecting invasive fungal infections.
